<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leeward Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Leeward Islands are the northern group of the Lesser Antilles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, from the Virgin Islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> settled several of them in the 17th century, and from 1671 the British islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>, Nevis, Antigua and Montserrat were governed together as the Leeward Islands colony, later a federation that lasted until 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leeward-islands.jpg" alt="Leeward Islands" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MaligneRange · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Indies_-_Island_Groups.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Leeward Islands lie in the Lesser Antilles, where the northeastern Caribbean Sea meets the western North Atlantic. They begin with the Virgin Islands, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, and extend southeast. Most were formed by volcanoes in the Lesser Antilles subduction zone, and some are still active; the highest point is La Grande Soufrière in Guadeloupe, at 1,467 metres.</p><p>The name comes from the age of sail. Leeward means downwind, and the trade winds blow chiefly from the southeast, so a ship from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> would meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a> and Martinique first, the islands most to windward. Early Spanish explorers called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and the islands to its west Sotavento, and the islands south and east of it Islas de Barlovento. The British later named Antigua, Montserrat and the islands to the north the Leeward Islands, whereas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, French and Spanish the whole chain is still called the windward islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Caribs, after whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> is named, are believed to have migrated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco River</a> area of Venezuela about 1200 CE. In the century before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> arrived in 1492 they mostly displaced the Maipurean-speaking Taínos, by warfare, extermination and assimilation. The islands were among the first parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to fall under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, and many of their names date from Columbus's second voyage: Montserrat was named for the Monastery of Montserrat in Catalonia.</p><p>The Leeward Islands became a British colony in 1671, with William Stapleton as its first governor. The settler population changed sharply. In 1660 there were about 8,000 white settlers and about 2,000 enslaved Africans; in 1678 there were 10,408 settlers and 8,449 enslaved people; and by 1708 the settlers numbered 7,311 and the enslaved an estimated 23,500. The colony was dissolved as a federation in 1816, when the islands were ruled individually, and re-established in 1833.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leeward Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the time of Spanish contact the Kalinago were among the dominant peoples of the Caribbean, living throughout the Windward Islands, Dominica and the southern <b>Leeward Islands</b>, including Guadeloupe, and possibly Barbados.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch buccaneers and settlers had a foothold on Tortola when English forces from the <b>Leeward Islands</b> seized it in 1672.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-virgin-islands">British Virgin Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>Tobago left for Trinidad in 1889, and Dominica joined from the <b>Leeward Islands</b> in 1940.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-windward-islands">British Windward Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>English Harbour, later Nelson's Dockyard, was the Royal Navy's chief base in the <b>Leewards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Colony of Antigua</a></li><li><blockquote>Part of the <b>Leeward Islands</b> until 1940 and then of the British Windward Islands, Dominica joined the West Indies Federation, became an Associated State in 1967 and gained independence as the Commonwealth of Dominica on 3 November 1978.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-dominica">Colony of Dominica</a></li><li><blockquote>It comprised Curaçao, Aruba and Bonaire off Venezuela, and Sint Maarten, Saba and Sint Eustatius in the <b>Leewards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/netherlands-antilles">Netherlands Antilles</a></li></ul></section>
